      "detail": "API-REV-008 Studio nested scope and provider brief briefId brief-8ce409e3-4b38-414c-80aa-46c5fd54d06c In_review Created 24.8.2026, 10:43:31 Updated 24.8.2026, 10:45:25 BRIEF RECORD brief-8ce409e3-4b38-414c-80aa-46c5fd54d06c Status in_review DRAFT OUTPUTS 2 1 final REVIEW 0 notes Approved — · Rejected — Generate draft Approve Reject Draft outputs Generated drafts stay attached to the same brief record for review. researcher Research /private/tmp/api-rev008-studio-clean-1787560810/applications/bundle-app__brief-studio__dff2993a20fe475cc984604995d2289c03130e4d46e5d81db3c2ac2211b740f7/runtime/brief-studio/research.md Concise research checkpoint on nested scope and provider resolution. # API-REV-008 Studio nested scope and provider brief ## Key findings - Nested scope should be modeled explicitly: a child scope inherits approved parent context by default, while local settings override only where declared. - Provider selection belongs to the narrowest scope that needs it; avoid silently promoting a child provider choice to the workspace or global level. - Resolution should be deterministic and inspectable: evaluate local configuration, then ancestors, then a documented default; expose the winning source and override chain. - Provider capabilities, credentials, rate limits, and data boundaries may differ by scope, so the effective provider contract must be computed per request. - The brief should distinguish configuration ownership (who may set a value) from runtime visibility (which nested scopes may read it). ## Risks or open questions - What happens when a child requests a provider unavailable or disallowed in an ancestor scope: fail closed, fall back, or require explicit approval? - How are conflicting policy controls (data residency, retention, model restrictions) merged across levels? - Can users audit inherited values and understand why a provider was selected without inspecting implementation details? ## Recommendations or next steps - Define a precedence matrix for global, workspace, project, and nested scopes, including explicit deny versus unset. - Add an effective-configuration endpoint/view that returns provider, source scope, policy decisions, and fallback status. - Validate scope/provider combinations at configuration time and re-check authorization at request time. - Test deep nesting, missing providers, policy conflicts, and rollback of inherited settings before rollout. writer Final /private/tmp/api-rev008-studio-clean-1787560810/applications/bundle-app__brief-studio__dff2993a20fe475cc984604995d2289c03130e4d46e5d81db3c2ac2211b740f7/runtime/brief-studio/final-brief.md Concise writer checkpoint with recommendation, evidence, cautions, and next actions. # API-REV-008: Make nested provider resolution inherited, local, and auditable **Recommendation.** Adopt inheritance-by-default for nested scopes, with narrow local overrides and explicit deny semantics. Select providers at the narrowest authorized scope that requires them, then compute and validate the effective provider contract for every request. Make the resolution chain visible so operators can explain which scope supplied each value, policy decision, and fallback. ## Key evidence - Child scopes should inherit approved parent context unless they declare an override; this avoids duplicated configuration while preserving local control. - Provider choices must remain local to the scope that owns the need; never silently promote a child selection to workspace or global configuration. - Resolution should be deterministic: evaluate local settings, walk ancestors in a documented order, then apply a documented default. - Capabilities, credentials, rate limits, retention/residency rules, and data boundaries can vary by scope; authorization cannot be assumed from provider identity alone. - Configuration ownership (who may set a value) is distinct from runtime visibility (which descendants may read it). ## Risks or cautions - An unavailable or disallowed child provider needs a defined fail-closed, fallback, or explicit-approval outcome; silent fallback can violate data-boundary or model restrictions. - Conflicting ancestor policies require a precedence/merge matrix, including how explicit deny differs from unset. - Inherited values that are not inspectable will make incidents and access reviews difficult to investigate. ## Next actions - Define precedence and deny/unset semantics for global, workspace, project, and nested scopes. - Add an effective-configuration view or endpoint returning the selected provider, source scope, override chain, policy decisions, and fallback status. - Validate scope/provider compatibility at configuration time and re-check authorization, credentials, capability, rate-limit, and data-boundary constraints at request time. - Test deep nesting, missing providers, policy conflicts, fallback behavior, inherited-setting rollback, and audit-log completeness before rollout. Review notes Keep the approval conversation on the brief record itself. No review notes yet. Add review note Workflow diagnostics"
